# Built-in ACME client: angie itself issues and renews certificates, without a
# second container.
# https://angie.software/angie/docs/configuration/modules/http/http_acme/
#
# Rendered at container start by 40-acme.sh. The module is static
# (--with-http_acme_module), so it needs no load_module. This snippet orders
# nothing by itself: issuance starts when a consumer server {} references the
# client with `acme` and uses `ssl_certificate $acme_cert_<name>`, see
# docs/configuration.md (ACME).

# Client state: account key, private keys and issued certificates. angie
# creates the directory itself, mode 0700, owned by the master's uid. It must
# survive container re-creation: on `--tmpfs /data` certificates are reordered
# on every start, and Let's Encrypt limits duplicates to 5 per week, so the
# failure shows up later and looks like a CA problem.
acme_client_path /data/acme;

# No `resolver` here on purpose: since Angie 1.12.0 its default is `conf`, i.e.
# /etc/resolv.conf of the container. Declaring it would be harmful: a second
# `resolver` in the same context is fatal (`"resolver" directive is duplicate`),
# and custom/http-conf.d shares this http {}. Conversely, your own
# `resolver off;` disables ACME too (`no resolver configured`).

# http-01 challenge port. It must equal the port of the image's built-in
# listener (ANGIE_HTTP_PORT: 80 root, 8080 unprivileged): the module then opens
# no listener of its own, and the challenge handler runs in the post_read phase
# of existing servers, even one doing `return 301` to https, since post_read
# precedes rewrite. The directive's own default is 80, which in the unprivileged
# image would open a separate listener and fail to bind under a non-root uid.
acme_http_port %%ACME_HTTP_PORT%%;

# The client is declared only when ANGIE_ACME_DIRECTORY is set. Otherwise
# declare your own in /etc/angie/custom/http-conf.d, with options the toggle
# lacks (key_type, profile, eab); the two directives above stay from the image
# either way, their values follow from its layout.
%%ACME_CLIENT%%
